1
00:06:25,714 --> 00:06:31,714
On air. Off shore. Out of control.

2
00:12:51,428 --> 00:12:57,428
The Boat That Rocked is a drama, comedy film directed by Richard Curtis.

3
00:19:17,142 --> 00:19:23,142
When a group of rebellious deejays decides to defy the ban on government-censored music, they
take to the seas to broadcast music and mayhem to millions of adoring fans.

4
00:25:42,857 --> 00:25:48,857
Carl, played by Tom Sturridge.

5
00:32:08,571 --> 00:32:14,571
The Count, played by Philip Seymour Hoffman.

6
00:38:34,285 --> 00:38:40,285
Gavin, played by Rhys Ifans.

7
00:45:00,000 --> 00:45:06,000
Quentin, played by Bill Nighy.

8
00:51:25,714 --> 00:51:31,714
Charlotte, played by Emma Thompson.

9
00:57:51,428 --> 00:57:57,428
Dave, played by Nick Frost.

10
01:04:17,142 --> 01:04:23,142
The Boat That Rocked (titled Pirate Radio in North America) is a 2009 comedy-drama written and
directed by Richard Curtis about pirate radio in the United Kingdom during the 1960s.

11
01:10:42,857 --> 01:10:48,857
The film has an ensemble cast consisting of Philip Seymour Hoffman,
Bill Nighy, Rhys Ifans, Nick Frost and Kenneth Branagh.

12
01:17:08,571 --> 01:17:14,571
Set in 1966, it tells the story of the fictional pirate radio station "Radio Rock" and its crew of eclectic disc jockeys, who broadcast rock
and pop music to the United Kingdom from a ship anchored in the North Sea while the British government tries to shut them down.

13
01:23:34,285 --> 01:23:40,285
It was produced by Working Title Films for Universal Pictures and was
filmed on the Isle of Portland and at Shepperton Studios.

14
01:30:00,000 --> 01:30:06,000
After the world premiere in Leicester Square on 23 March 2009, the film was
released theatrically in the United Kingdom and Ireland on 1 April 2009.

15
01:36:25,714 --> 01:36:31,714
It was a commercial failure at the British box office making only US$10.1 million in its
first three months, just a fifth of its US$50 million production cost.

16
01:42:51,428 --> 01:42:57,428
It received mixed reviews and criticism for its length.

17
01:49:17,142 --> 01:49:23,142
For its North American release, the film was cut by
20 minutes and retitled Pirate Radio.

18
01:55:42,857 --> 01:55:48,857
Opening on 13 November 2009, its worldwide cinema run finished in
January 2010; the film had grossed US$36.6 million.

19
02:02:08,571 --> 02:02:14,571
The film explores themes of ship, rock 'n' roll,
great britain, radio station, north sea.

20
02:08:34,285 --> 02:08:40,285
The tone is lighthearted and emotional.
